consiSt("vi",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of the Virgin Islands","d":"The territory’s court of last resort, sitting in regular sessions on St. Croix and elsewhere as ordered. Hears appeals of right from final Superior Court judgments and listed interlocutory orders, permissive interlocutory appeals, original writs (mandamus, prohibition, habeas in exceptional cases), and certified questions from federal and state courts. It also controls bar admission (including pro hac vice), discipline, and all court rules through promulgation orders. Cases are heard by three-justice panels; en banc review is available under Rule 40 (Dec. 1, 2024)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURT","n":"Superior Court of the Virgin Islands","d":"The single general-jurisdiction trial court, with two judicial districts (St. Thomas/St. John and St. Croix). Divisions include Civil, Criminal, Family, Probate, Small Claims, Traffic, and a Complex Litigation Division (V.I. R. Civ. P. 92–96) for mass-tort, disaster, class, and similar cases."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Magistrate Division of the Superior Court","d":"Magistrate judges hear the matters assigned to them by statute (original jurisdiction) and pretrial matters a judge designates, and may try any civil case, jury or non-jury, if all parties consent within 15 days after the pleadings close. A final decision in an original-jurisdiction case is appealed within 14 days to a Superior Court judge (the Appellate Division); a judgment in a consent case goes straight to the Supreme Court."},{"l":"FEDERAL (SEPARATE)","n":"District Court of the Virgin Islands","d":"An Article IV federal court, not part of the territorial Judicial Branch and not covered by this package. It is already covered separately in Consi. Cases removed to it and remanded can bring a District Court pro hac vice admission back into Superior Court (V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(d)(3))."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute found, but the Code was not checked directly","d":"No anti-SLAPP law was found. The Legislature’s official bill tracker (billtracking.legvi.org) was searched across every legislature it holds (roughly the 28th through 36th) for “SLAPP,” “strategic lawsuit,” “public participation,” and “defamation.” None of those searches returned a record. A search for “free speech” turned up only a 2013 request for a legal opinion.\nThe collected court rules contain no special motion to dismiss, no discovery stay, and no fee-shifting tied to speech or petitioning. The only official online copy of the Virgin Islands Code that legvi.org links to is hosted on LexisNexis, which this package does not use. That means the Code itself was not searched.\nTreat this as unverified. Before advising a client, check the Code (Titles 5 and 14) and recent session laws. Separately, Virgin Islands law already has general prevailing-party fee-shifting (5 V.I.C. § 541, through V.I. R. Civ. P. 54(d)(2)), so a defendant who wins a meritless speech suit may recover fees on that basis anyway.","c":"None verified; bill-tracker search (Nov. 10, 2026); V.I. R. Civ. P. 54(d)(2)","v":false}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: Supreme Court Rule 201, on a local lawyer’s motion","d":"A lawyer not regularly or specially admitted in the Virgin Islands may be admitted pro hac vice, in the Supreme Court’s discretion, for one matter only. The motion must be made by a regularly admitted Virgin Islands lawyer. The applicant must:\n• be an active member in good standing of the bar of any state, territory, or foreign country;\n• never have been disbarred or suspended anywhere;\n• have been retained or asked to represent a party in a Virgin Islands matter; and\n• pay Supreme Court and V.I. Bar Association dues and register under Rule 203(e).\nA “matter” includes arbitrations, mediations, and transactions, not just lawsuits. The admitted lawyer is bound by the V.I. Rules of Professional Conduct and is subject to the Court’s discipline and contempt power even after the admission ends.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(a)","v":true},{"t":"What to file and what it costs","d":"File the motion with the Court’s Pro Hac Vice Questionnaire, served on counsel for all parties with certificates of service. The application fee is $350: a $100 Clerk’s filing fee plus a $250 Committee of Bar Examiners fee. Both can be paid through VIJEFS or by certified check or money order.\nThe checklist also requires:\n• a supporting affidavit from the moving lawyer;\n• a proposed order;\n• certificates of good standing from the highest court of every jurisdiction where the applicant is admitted, dated no more than 60 days before filing; and\n• proof that separate per-case dues were paid directly to the V.I. Bar Association. A letter from a bar association is not enough.\nThe dues amount is not stated in the collected texts.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(b); PHV Checklist ¶¶ 1–7; Bar Application Fee Schedule","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must sign every pleading and answers for the case","d":"Every pleading filed by a pro hac vice lawyer must be signed by local counsel. If it is not, the pleading will be rejected. When filing electronically, this is satisfied by local counsel’s electronic signature or by a statement that local counsel reviewed and consented to the filing.\nThe regularly admitted lawyer of record is accountable to the Supreme Court for prosecuting the matter on time and following the rules. That lawyer must notify the Court within 14 days after the matter ends or the pro hac vice lawyer withdraws.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(a), (c); V.I. R. App. P. 40.2(b)(3) (2011 e-filing rule)","v":true},{"t":"One admission per client per matter; appeals and remands are covered","d":"An existing admission carries over when the same matter is appealed to the Supreme Court or remanded. It also carries over when the matter is remanded from the District Court, assigned to a Rule 93 master case, consolidated (if the lawyer does no work on the other matters), or certified as a class the client belongs to. Collateral writ proceedings are not covered.\nA new admission, with new fees for each matter, is required when:\n• the admission has ended;\n• the lawyer takes on another client, even in the same matter;\n• the lawyer represents the same client in a different matter;\n• the case is dismissed and refiled under a new number; or\n• a class is decertified.\nA lawyer seeking a repeat admission may rely on earlier good-standing certificates by swearing that nothing has changed.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(d)(1), (2), (4)","v":true},{"t":"Remand from the District Court: 15-day notice","d":"A lawyer admitted pro hac vice by the District Court of the Virgin Islands may keep representing the same client after a remand to Superior Court without a new application. To do so, the sponsoring lawyer must file a notice with the Supreme Court Clerk within 15 days after the remand order. The notice must include the District Court admission and remand orders, the Superior Court docket number, proof that pro hac vice dues were paid to the V.I. Bar Association, and the lawyer’s affidavit consenting to discipline. If the case was dismissed rather than remanded, the District Court admission does not continue.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(d)(3)","v":true},{"t":"New annual registration and $300 fee for pro hac vice lawyers from 2027","d":"Supreme Court Rule 214 takes effect January 1, 2027. It requires every active member of the Virgin Islands Bar, expressly including lawyers admitted only pro hac vice, to e-file an annual attorney registration statement and pay $300 between January 1 and January 31. Late filing is allowed through February 28 with a $200 late fee. The statement includes certifications on Virgin Islands taxes, child support, trust-account and client-file rules, a succession plan for private practitioners, and CLE compliance.\nThe same order strikes the old Rule 203(e) registration requirement, which carried a $50 annual fee, as of that date.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 214.2(a)–(d) (Prom. 2026-0006, eff. Jan. 1, 2027)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing is mandatory, and pro hac vice admission comes first","d":"All documents in Superior Court and Supreme Court cases must be filed through the Virgin Islands Judiciary Electronic Filing System (VIJEFS). Every admitted lawyer must register, including pro hac vice, special, and limited-license admittees and their staff. An out-of-territory lawyer must be admitted pro hac vice before registering.\nA filing is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. Atlantic Standard Time on the due date. Exemptions are disfavored and require a motion.","c":"V.I. E-Filing R. 3(a), 4(a)(3), 5(c), 7(a)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation is allowed, but withdrawal needs leave of court","d":"A lawyer may limit the scope of a representation if the limit is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. Short-term limited services through nonprofit or court-annexed programs get relaxed conflict rules.\nThe collected civil rules have no limited-appearance procedure. Once counsel appears, withdrawal requires leave of court after notice to the client. The motion must include a verified statement about contact with the client and updated litigant data forms. Treat any appearance as an appearance for the whole case unless the court orders otherwise.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 211.1.2(c), 211.6.5; V.I. R. Civ. P. 6-5","v":true},{"t":"No out-of-territory practice without admission","d":"The Virgin Islands version of Rule 5.5 has only paragraphs (a) and (b). It omits the ABA Model Rule’s safe harbors for temporary practice. A lawyer not admitted in the Virgin Islands may not set up an office or a systematic and continuous presence there except as allowed by 4 V.I.C. § 443 or other law, and may not hold out as admitted there.\nBecause Rule 201 treats transactions, arbitrations, and mediations as “matters,” plan on pro hac vice admission for any Virgin Islands work. The text of 4 V.I.C. § 443 was not collected.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 211.5.5; V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(a)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Supreme Court Rule 211 (V.I. Rules of Professional Conduct)","d":"The Virgin Islands Rules of Professional Conduct are Supreme Court Rule 211, numbered 211.1.0 through 211.8.5 to track the ABA Model Rules (for example, Model Rule 1.6 is Rule 211.1.6). The collected version (posted April 2021) has no official comments. Pro hac vice lawyers are bound by these rules, and the attorney oath requires following them.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 211; V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(a); V.I. S. Ct. R. 204(i)(3)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 5.5 has no temporary-practice safe harbors","d":"Rule 211.5.5 contains only (a), which bars unauthorized practice, and (b), which bars an office or continuous presence and holding out. It has none of Model Rule 5.5(c)–(e): no temporary practice tied to a pending or expected proceeding, no ADR or transactional safe harbor, and no in-house or federal-law exceptions. Statutory exceptions are left to 4 V.I.C. § 443, which was not collected.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 211.5.5","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: no conflict-check exception and no Rule 1.6(c)","d":"Rule 211.1.6(b) lists six permitted disclosures (harm, crime or fraud, rectification, ethics advice, the lawyer’s own claims or defenses, and court order or other law). It omits Model Rule 1.6(b)(7), which allows disclosure to detect conflicts when a lawyer changes firms. It also has no counterpart to Model Rule 1.6(c), the duty to make reasonable efforts to prevent unauthorized disclosure.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 211.1.6","v":true},{"t":"No screening of lateral hires; no humanitarian gifts; no Rule 8.4(g)","d":"• Rule 211.1.10(a) imputes a conflict to the whole firm unless it rests on a personal interest. It does not include the Model Rule’s screening exception for lateral lawyers. Screening appears only for prospective clients (211.1.18(d)) and government lawyers (211.1.11).\n• Rule 211.1.8(e) lets a lawyer advance litigation costs, or pay them for an indigent client. It does not include the Model Rule’s 2020 exception for modest humanitarian gifts to pro bono clients.\n• Rule 211.8.4 runs (a) through (f) only, with no Model Rule 8.4(g) harassment-and-discrimination provision.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 211.1.10(a), 211.1.8(e), 211.8.4","v":true},{"t":"Client files: 10-year retention and a defined ‘file’","d":"Rule 211.1.15-2 is a Virgin Islands addition. The client’s file does not include the lawyer’s notes, research, and internal memos, or unfiled drafts unless the client paid for them. The lawyer must turn the file over on request and may charge only limited copying costs.\nThe file must be kept for 10 years after the representation ends, unless delivered to the client or the client authorizes destruction in a signed writing. After 10 years it may be destroyed on reasonable notice. Criminal files in death, life, or indeterminate-sentence cases must be kept for the client’s life.\nLawyers should prepare a succession plan naming successor counsel. From 2027, Rule 214 requires private practitioners to certify that they have one.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 211.1.15-2(a)–(h); V.I. S. Ct. R. 214.2(b)(I)","v":true},{"t":"Detailed trust-account and IOLTA rules","d":"Beyond Rule 211.1.15, which requires 5-year retention of trust records, the Virgin Islands adds rules on:\n• specific trust-account records (211.1.15-1);\n• account requirements (211.1.15-3);\n• use of trust accounts (211.1.15-4);\n• approved institutions (211.1.15-5); and\n• the IOLTA program (211.1.15-6).\nReview them before handling client money in the territory. From 2027, each active lawyer must certify annually that they have reviewed Rules 211.1.15-1 and -2.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 211.1.15, 211.1.15-1 to -6; V.I. S. Ct. R. 214.2(b)(H)","v":true},{"t":"Candor about Virgin Islands law is a filing duty","d":"In the Superior Court, signing a paper certifies that the applicable Virgin Islands law has been cited, including authority for and against the party’s position. This duty is in V.I. R. Civ. P. 11(b)(5) and goes beyond federal Rule 11.\nIn the Supreme Court, a lawyer who appears to mislead by citing other jurisdictions’ law when Virgin Islands law points the other way, or who fails to present controlling contrary law, is subject to sanctions.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 11(b)(5); V.I. R. App. P. 15(b)","v":true}],"style":"Territory of the United States Virgin Islands","home":"https://www.vicourts.org/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Short deadlines skip weekends and holidays in Superior Court","d":"In Superior Court, any period of 14 days or less excludes intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays. Periods of 15 days or more count every day. This is the pre-2009 federal method, not today’s federal rule. The Supreme Court’s Rule 16(b) has no such exclusion, so the same “14 days” can end on different dates in the two courts. Use the Virgin Islands holiday list.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 6(a)(1)(B); V.I. R. App. P. 16(b)"},{"t":"Cite Virgin Islands law, including adverse authority, in every paper","d":"Rule 11(b)(5) makes citing the applicable Virgin Islands law, both for and against your position, part of the Rule 11 certification. The Supreme Court can reject arguments that lack adequate citation and sanctions lawyers who rely on other jurisdictions’ law when Virgin Islands law points the other way. Supreme Court briefs must also cite authorities in a set order of precedence, with parallel citations to the V.I. Reports.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 11(b)(5); V.I. R. App. P. 15(b), 22(i)"},{"t":"Fee shifting: the loser may pay your opponent’s lawyer","d":"The prevailing party may recover attorney’s fees, except in personal-injury cases without frivolousness. Serve and file the verified, itemized bill of costs with invoices within 30 days after judgment, or costs may be waived. On appeal, a separate verified bill of costs is due within 14 days after the Supreme Court’s judgment. Any attorney’s fees component is decided by the Superior Court on remand.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 54(d)(1)(A), (C), (E), (2)(A); V.I. R. App. P. 30(b)"},{"t":"No waiver of service; 120 days to serve; out-of-territory defendants get 30 days","d":"Rule 4(d) is reserved, so there is no federal-style waiver-of-service procedure. Serve within 120 days after filing, not 90. Out-of-territory service follows 5 V.I.C. § 4911, and a defendant served that way has 30 days to answer, not 21. The Government of the Virgin Islands has 30 days after service on the Governor and Attorney General. Its employees sued individually have 45 days.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 4(d), 4(n), 12(a)(1)(B), 12(a)(2)–(3)"},{"t":"Motion format traps","d":"Every motion, response, and reply:\n• is limited to the greater of 20 pages or 6,000 words;\n• must carry a compliance statement in the certificate of service; and\n• if it seeks affirmative relief, must come with a proposed order filed in Word format (not locked PDF) through VIJEFS.\nResponses are due in 14 days, 20 days for Rule 12 motions. Further filings after the reply need leave. Extension motions must state whether opposing counsel agrees and how many extensions have already been sought.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 6-1(a)(4), (c), (e), (f), 6-2; V.I. E-Filing R. 7(a)(6)"},{"t":"Discovery motions need a letter and a live conference","d":"Before any discovery motion other than one about depositions, the moving party must serve a letter specifying each disputed request and the order sought. The lawyers must then confer in person if practicable, otherwise by phone or video, within 15 days. Exchanging letters or emails does not satisfy the rule.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 37-1"},{"t":"Summary judgment: numbered fact statements and a 30-day response","d":"The motion must contain a separately numbered statement of undisputed facts with record citations. The opposition, due 30 days after filing, must respond to each numbered fact. The reply is due 14 days later and must answer any additional facts. Unless the court sets a different time, a motion is untimely if filed more than 30 days after all discovery closes.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 56(b), 56(c)(1)–(3)"},{"t":"Continuances need the client’s signature and 7 days’ notice","d":"Since July 1, 2026, a continuance request must be in writing, filed at least 7 days before the event, and signed by both the attorney and the party. Agreement of counsel, incomplete discovery, new counsel, an unsubpoenaed witness, and likely settlement are listed as generally not good cause. Any trial continuance beyond the second trial setting is also disfavored.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 111(b)–(c); V.I. R. Civ. P. 6-3(d)"},{"t":"File the notice of appeal in the Supreme Court and watch the 120-day rule","d":"The notice of appeal is filed with the Supreme Court Clerk, served conventionally, and mailed to the trial judge. The deadline is 30 days after entry, or 60 if the Virgin Islands or U.S. government is a party.\nTime runs from docket entry even if no separate judgment document is entered. A tolling motion that the Superior Court has not decided within 120 days is deemed denied, which starts a new 30 days. A fee motion does not toll.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 4(a), (d), (f); 5(a)(1), (4), (9)"},{"t":"Appellate extension motions are due 5 working days early","d":"Supreme Court deadlines are strictly construed. A motion to extend any deadline other than for a notice of appeal must be filed at least 5 working days before the deadline and served at least 3 working days before. Missed deadlines can lead to dismissal without further notice.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 17"},{"t":"Pro hac vice is per client, per matter, and local counsel signs everything","d":"Each additional client, even in the same case, and each new matter needs a new admission with new fees ($100 + $250) and per-case V.I. Bar dues. Local counsel must sign every pleading. From January 1, 2027, pro hac vice lawyers also owe the annual Rule 214 registration and $300 fee. Rule 5.5 offers no temporary-practice safe harbor, so get admitted before doing transactional or ADR work.","c":"V.I. S. Ct. R. 201(b)–(d); 211.5.5; 214.2(a), (c)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory substitution of judge in the collected rules","d":"None of the collected civil, appellate, or Superior Court rules gives a party a peremptory right to substitute or challenge a judge. Disqualification is for cause under 4 V.I.C. § 284, which the rules cross-reference (for example, for masters and mediators) but which was not collected.\nThe Superior Court can reassign a case to a judge from the other district when a judge or court employee from one district is a party, except in uncontested matters.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 53(a)(2), 90(e)(4); Super. Ct. R. 17","v":true},{"t":"Magistrate judges: listed grounds and on-the-record disclosure","d":"A magistrate judge may not sit if the magistrate judge:\n• is a party or primarily interested;\n• is related to a party or a corporate party’s officer within the third degree;\n• was counsel in the same or a related matter; or\n• cannot give a fair hearing because of probable bias.\nA magistrate judge who knows of a ground must declare it in open court and state the reason, with the memorandum sent to parties not present, and must comply with 4 V.I.C. § 284. The case then goes to the other magistrate judge in the district, then to one from the other district, then to a judge.","c":"Super. Ct. R. 318, 318.1, 318.2","v":true},{"t":"Supreme Court: appellant must flag possible disqualification","d":"If any justice, or a designated justice on the panel, took part in the case or a related proceeding in the Superior Court, the appellant must promptly notify the Clerk in writing after filing the notice of appeal and copy the appellee. The appellee must do so if the appellant does not. En banc votes count only justices who are not disqualified, and an en banc decision needs at least three justices to agree.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 19; V.I. R. App. P. 40(a), (g), (h) (Prom. 2024-0001)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Revised Organic Act bill of rights: broader than federal not established here","d":"The Virgin Islands has no constitution of its own. Section 3 of the Revised Organic Act of 1954 (48 U.S.C. § 1561) carries a bill of rights that extends the First Amendment to the territory. Neither that text nor any case law reading it was collected, so whether Virgin Islands courts give speech broader protection than the First Amendment was not checked.","c":"Revised Organic Act § 3 (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Its own holiday calendar affects every deadline","d":"Legal holidays in the Virgin Islands include several that mainland lawyers will not expect:\n• Three Kings Day (Jan. 6)\n• Transfer Day (Mar. 31)\n• Holy Thursday, Good Friday, and Easter Monday\n• Emancipation Day (July 3)\n• Columbus Day and Puerto Rico Friendship Day\n• D. Hamilton Jackson Day (Nov. 1)\n• Election Day\n• Christmas Second Day (Dec. 26)\nJuneteenth was added in 2023.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 6(a); V.I. R. App. P. 16(b) (both as amended by Prom. 2023-0003)","v":true},{"t":"Notice pleading and no dollar demand","d":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 8(a)(2) expressly says the Virgin Islands is a notice-pleading jurisdiction. The Reporter’s Note explains that this rejects the federal plausibility standard. Under 5 V.I.C. § 5, the prayer must say damages are within the court’s jurisdictional limit and must not state a dollar amount.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 8(a)(2)–(3) & Reporter’s Note","v":true},{"t":"Prevailing-party attorney’s fees","d":"Unlike the American rule, 5 V.I.C. § 541, as restated in Rule 54(d)(2), lets the court award the prevailing party a discretionary amount for attorney’s fees, as indemnity. Fees are barred in personal-injury cases unless the complaint or defense was frivolous. Costs and fees go in a verified bill of costs with invoices, due within 30 days after judgment. If the prevailing party does not comply, costs may be waived.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 54(d)(1)–(2)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Virgin Islands Rules of Appellate Procedure (V.I. R. App. P.), Judiciary compilation (restated by Prom. 2017-004, posted Nov. 2019), with Prom. 2023-0003 (Rule 16(b), eff. Sept. 1, 2023) and Prom. 2024-0001 (new Rule 40 en banc and replacement Rule 31, eff. Dec. 1, 2024). Interim Virgin Islands Electronic Filing Rules (Prom. 2020-0004, as corrected Aug. 14, 2020; Rule 7(a)(6) amended 2023). Rules Governing the Superior Court (amended June 1, 2019), Rules 321–322. V.I. R. Civ. P. 6-4, 50, 52, 59, 60, 62, 91. Supreme Court Clerk fee schedule (web, Nov. 2026). Official texts from vicourts.org and supreme.vicourts.org; summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in the Virgin Islands: one appellate court, a 30-day clock, and a 120-day trap","d":"The Supreme Court of the Virgin Islands hears appeals from final judgments of the Superior Court, from interlocutory orders that 4 V.I.C. § 33(b)–(c) makes appealable, and from orders a Superior Court judge certifies for permissive appeal. There is no intermediate appellate court. Decisions of the Magistrate Division in its original-jurisdiction cases go first to a Superior Court judge, sitting as the Appellate Division, within 14 days. Agency decisions are reviewed by writ of review in the Superior Court.\nIn a civil case, file the notice of appeal with the Supreme Court Clerk within 30 days after the judgment is entered on the docket, or 60 days if the Virgin Islands government or the United States is a party. Timely post-judgment motions restart the clock, but a motion undecided for 120 days is deemed denied. Briefs are word-limited (7,800 and 3,900 words), and extension motions must be filed at least 5 working days early."},"groups":[["Which route",[{"t":"Appeal of right to the Supreme Court","d":"An appeal permitted by law goes from the Superior Court to the Supreme Court. To be appealable as of right, an order must be final or fall within the interlocutory categories in 4 V.I.C. § 33(b) and (c). That statute was not collected.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 4(a), 5(a)(2)"},{"t":"Magistrate Division decisions go to a Superior Court judge first","d":"Final orders of the Magistrate Division in its original-jurisdiction cases, and interlocutory orders appealable by law, are appealed to a Superior Court judge. File the notice with the Superior Court Clerk within 14 days after entry. It must include a position statement of no more than 780 words (3 pages if self-represented). The filing fee, or an IFP motion, is due within 14 days, and any response is due within 14 days.\nA judgment entered by a magistrate judge in a civil case tried by consent goes directly to the Supreme Court.","c":"Super. Ct. R. 322(a)–(b); Super. Ct. R. 321(c)(4)"},{"t":"Agency decisions: writ of review in the Superior Court","d":"Review of a decision by a statutory officer, board, commission, or tribunal is by petition for writ of review, filed with the Superior Court’s Appellate Division within the statutory time. If no time is set by statute, file within 30 days after the decision.\nThe agency returns the writ and record within 20 days. The petitioner’s brief is due 20 days after the record or briefing schedule, and the respondent’s brief 20 days after that. A reply is allowed only if ordered.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 91(a), (d), (f)–(g)"},{"t":"Certified questions and transfers","d":"The Supreme Court may answer questions of Virgin Islands law certified by any U.S. court or a state or territorial court of last resort. It accepts or rejects within 60 days, and a request not granted within 60 days is deemed denied. Briefs follow at 40 days and then 30 days.\nApplications under 4 V.I.C. § 32(d) to transfer a case to the Supreme Court must show issues of public importance urgent enough that normal appeal would be inadequate.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 38(e)(2), (g); 39(a)"}]],["Filing and serving the notice of appeal",[{"t":"File in the Supreme Court, not the trial court","d":"The notice of appeal is filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court, through VIJEFS. A notice filed by mistake in the Superior Court is forwarded and treated as filed on the date the Superior Court received it. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 4(a), 5(a)(1), 5(a)(11); V.I. E-Filing R. 13(a)(1)"},{"t":"Contents and appendix-to-notice duties","d":"The notice must:\n• name the appealing parties, with their physical addresses and telephone numbers;\n• designate the judgment or order appealed; and\n• state the reasons or issues to be presented.\nDefects of form do not defeat the appeal, but omissions of substance can draw sanctions. In a civil case, failing to take any step other than the timely notice is not jurisdictional, but can lead to dismissal.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 4(a), (c)"},{"t":"Serve conventionally and mail a copy to the trial judge","d":"Serve the notice conventionally on every other party, even if it was e-filed, and file a certificate of service. Notices of appeal are “initiating documents,” which the E-Filing Rules require to be served by conventional methods unless the other side consented to e-service.\nMail a copy to the trial judge by ordinary mail. Within 15 days, the judge may file a written opinion or amplify an earlier ruling.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 4(d), (f); V.I. E-Filing R. 2(k), 6(b)"},{"t":"Docket fee","d":"Each notice must be accompanied by the docket fee, which the Supreme Court Clerk lists as $105 for filing and docketing an appeal, unless IFP is granted. If an appeal is docketed without the fee, it must be paid within 14 days after docketing. Otherwise the Clerk may dismiss without further notice unless a supported IFP motion is pending. Parties filing separate notices each pay a fee. A joint notice means one fee, one brief, and one appendix.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 3(a), 4(b), 4(e); Supreme Court Clerk fee schedule"},{"t":"Tell the Court about possible disqualification","d":"If any justice took part in the case or a related proceeding below, the appellant must promptly notify the Clerk in writing after filing the notice, with a copy to the appellee.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 19"}]],["When the clock starts and what stops it",[{"t":"30 days (60 with a government party) from docket entry","d":"In a civil case, the deadline is 30 days after the date of entry. It is 60 days for any party if the Government of the Virgin Islands, the United States, or an officer or agency of either is a party. A judgment is entered when it is entered on the docket under Superior Court practice. Failure to enter a separate document does not toll the time.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(1), 5(a)(9)"},{"t":"Tolling motions and the 120-day deemed denial","d":"A timely motion for judgment as a matter of law, to amend findings, for a new trial, to alter or amend, or for relief from judgment (if filed within 28 days) extends the appeal time for all parties to 30 days after entry of the order disposing of the last such motion.\nIf the Superior Court does not rule within 120 days after the motion was filed, the motion is deemed denied for appeal purposes. A motion for attorney’s fees does not affect the time.\nTrial-level deadlines for these motions are all 28 days after entry of judgment and cannot be extended. The rules are V.I. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), and 59(b) and (e).","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4); V.I. R. Civ. P. 6(b)(2), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)"},{"t":"Premature notices and amending the notice","d":"A notice filed while a tolling motion is pending becomes effective only when the last motion is decided, or 120 days after it was filed. To challenge the ruling on the motion, or an amended judgment, file a notice or amended notice within the Rule 5 time measured from that ruling. No extra fee is charged.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4)"},{"t":"Keep the Supreme Court informed","d":"The appellant must promptly notify the Supreme Court of any pending trial-court motion and file the ruling within 14 days after it is made. If 120 days pass without a ruling, say so in the notice of appeal or within 14 days after the 120th day. Sanctions are possible for failure.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(6)"},{"t":"Late motions after 28 days","d":"A Rule 60 motion filed more than 28 days after judgment does not toll the time. While an appeal is pending, the Superior Court may deny it but cannot grant it. It must instead ask the Supreme Court to remand. The same applies to untimely or deemed-denied tolling motions.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(5)"},{"t":"Interlocutory reconsideration is a different clock","d":"Motions to reconsider non-final rulings are due within 14 days after entry under Civil Rule 6-4. They are not among the Rule 5(a)(4) tolling motions.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 6-4(a); V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4)"}]],["Extensions and reopening",[{"t":"Extension for excusable neglect or good cause","d":"The Superior Court may extend the time to appeal on a motion filed no later than 30 days after the original deadline expires. The extension may not exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later. If the Superior Court does not act within 120 days, the Supreme Court may decide the motion itself. Rule 2 cannot be used to enlarge appeal time.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(8); 2"},{"t":"Reopening when notice of entry was not received","d":"If a party did not receive notice of entry and no party would be substantially prejudiced, the Superior Court may reopen the time for 14 days. The motion must be filed within 90 days after entry or within 14 days after receiving notice, whichever is earlier. Parties still must monitor their cases.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(10)"},{"t":"Statutory deadlines cannot be extended","d":"Where a statute sets the time to appeal or seek relief, the rules’ extension provisions do not apply.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 1(i)"}]],["Cross-appeals and multiple parties",[{"t":"14 days after the first notice","d":"Once one party files a timely notice, any other party may file a notice within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the otherwise applicable time, whichever ends later.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(3)"},{"t":"Who is the appellant","d":"In a cross-appeal, the first party to file a notice is the appellant for briefing and argument. If the notices were filed the same day, the plaintiff below is the appellant. The appellee’s brief covers both its response and its cross-appeal, and the appellee may file a reply on the cross-appeal issues. Cross and separate appeals are argued together.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 22(c), (g); 27(d)"}]],["Interlocutory and expedited appeals",[{"t":"Permissive appeal of a certified order: 10 days","d":"If a Superior Court judge certifies that an order involves a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion, and that immediate appeal may materially advance the case, petition the Supreme Court within 10 days after entry. If the order is amended to add the certification, petition within 14 days after the amendment.\nThe petition may not exceed 2,600 words. An answer of up to 1,300 words is due within 10 days after service. If permission is granted, pay the fee and any cost bond within 14 days, and the petition serves as the notice of appeal.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 6(a), (c), (e)"},{"t":"Every interlocutory appeal must ask for expedition","d":"In all interlocutory appeals the appellant must move for expedited review under Rule 5(e), or face sanctions. That motion is due within 14 days after the notice of appeal and must state the exceptional reason. Any opposition is due within 10 days. Juvenile-transfer appeals must also seek expedition.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 6(b), 5(e), 5(d)"},{"t":"Stays pending appeal: ask the Superior Court first","d":"A request for a stay, supersedeas bond, or injunction pending appeal ordinarily goes first to the Superior Court. A motion to the Supreme Court must show that asking below was impracticable or was denied, and must attach the relevant record. It is normally decided by a three-justice panel, or by the Chief Justice in emergencies.\nIn Superior Court, a judgment is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry, except injunctions and receiverships. A party may obtain a stay at any time by bond or other security. The Virgin Islands government is exempt from bond to the extent 5 V.I.C. § 1141 provides.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 8(b)–(c); V.I. R. Civ. P. 62(a), (b), (e)"},{"t":"Writs of mandamus and prohibition","d":"A petition directed to a Superior Court judge is filed in the Supreme Court and served on the judge, all parties, and the Superior Court Clerk. It is titled “In re [petitioner]” and may not exceed 5,200 words. If an answer is ordered, it is limited to 5,200 words. Writ proceedings get preference over ordinary civil cases.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 13(a)–(b)"}]],["Record and transcripts",[{"t":"Order transcripts within 14 days on the TPO form","d":"Within 14 days after the notice of appeal (or after the order resolving a tolling motion), order the needed transcripts on the Transcript Purchase Order (TPO) form. If you will argue that a finding is unsupported, you must include all relevant evidence. File a statement of issues unless the entire transcript is ordered.\nThe appellee may designate more within 14 days. If the appellant does not order those parts within 14 days, the appellee may order them or move to compel within the next 14 days. Payment arrangements must be made when ordering.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 10(b)(1)–(3)"},{"t":"No transcript: statement of the evidence","d":"Serve a statement of the evidence within 14 days after the notice of appeal. Objections are due 14 days after service. Disputes are referred to the Superior Court to settle.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 10(c)"},{"t":"Electronic record","d":"The Superior Court Clerk files the electronic record or a hyperlink to it, with a certification that it is accurate and complete. Court reporters file transcripts with the Superior Court Clerk for transmission. Under the older Rule 40.3(j), the e-record was due 10 days after the last transcript, or 28 days after the notice if there was no transcript.","c":"V.I. E-Filing R. 13(a)(1)–(2); V.I. R. App. P. 40.3(j) (2011)"}]],["Briefs: schedule, length, and form",[{"t":"40 / 30 / 14 days","d":"The appellant’s brief is due 40 days after the record is complete or the briefing schedule issues, whichever is earlier. The appellee’s brief is due 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief, and the reply 14 days after service of the appellee’s brief.\nIf the appellant misses its deadline, the appellee may move to dismiss. An appellee who misses its deadline is not heard at argument without permission. Amicus briefs are due with the brief of the party supported and need consent of all parties or leave.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 25(a), (c); 23"},{"t":"Word limits and certificate","d":"Principal briefs are limited to 7,800 words and replies to 3,900, excluding the tables. A word-count certificate is required. Unrepresented parties who are not registered filers may use pages instead, at 260 words per page (for example, 30 pages for a principal brief). Other limits: a motion for permission to appeal is 2,600 words, a writ petition 5,200, and rehearing and en banc petitions 3,900.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 22(f), 15(h), 6(c), 13(a), 31(d)(6), 40(b)(2)"},{"t":"Required contents","d":"The appellant’s brief must include a jurisdictional statement with the filing dates that show timeliness. For each issue it must give the record pages where the issue was raised and ruled on, and the standard of review. It must also include a statement of related cases, a statement of the case with record citations, the argument, and the precise relief sought.\nOnly issues fairly presented below are reviewed unless the interests of justice require otherwise. Cite authorities in this order: U.S. Supreme Court, Third Circuit, V.I. Supreme Court, Superior Court, District Court of the V.I., then others. Use parallel citations to the V.I. Reports, and distinguish opposing local authority.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 22(a), (i); 4(h)"},{"t":"Appendix","d":"The appellant files a joint appendix with the brief. It must include the notice of appeal, the order appealed, the certified docket entries, and the relevant record. If sufficiency of the evidence is challenged, it must contain all evidence supporting the challenged finding. The parties should exchange designations within 14 days of each other.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 24(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Format","d":"Use at least 14-point type, including footnotes, with double spacing, 1-inch side margins, and three-quarter-inch top and bottom margins. On paper copies, covers are blue for the appellant, red for the appellee, green for an intervenor or amicus, gray for a reply, and white for the appendix. Cover colors do not apply to the e-filed version.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 15(a); V.I. E-Filing R. 13(a)(4)"}]],["Motions and extensions in the Supreme Court",[{"t":"14-day responses; proposed order required","d":"A response to a motion is due 14 days after service. Every motion must be accompanied by a proposed order with a distribution list. Procedural motions may be decided without waiting for a response, and a single justice may decide non-dispositive motions. No oral argument on motions unless ordered.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 21(a)–(c), (f)"},{"t":"Extensions: 5 working days before the deadline","d":"Deadlines are strictly construed, and a missed one can lead to dismissal without notice. A motion to extend a deadline, other than for a notice of appeal, must be filed at least 5 working days before it and received by the other parties at least 3 working days before. Any opposition is due at least 1 working day before the original deadline.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 17"},{"t":"Computing time","d":"In the Supreme Court, exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a weekend, a legal holiday, or a day the Clerk’s office is inaccessible. Intermediate weekends are counted. Add 3 days after service by mail, but nothing after electronic service. Holidays follow 1 V.I.C. § 171, including Three Kings Day, Transfer Day, Holy Thursday, Easter Monday, Juneteenth, D. Hamilton Jackson Day, and Christmas Second Day.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 16(b)–(d) (as amended by Prom. 2023-0003)"}]],["Rehearing and en banc review (effective Dec. 1, 2024)",[{"t":"Panel rehearing: 14 days, 3,900 words","d":"A petition for panel rehearing may be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment. It must state with particularity each point of law or fact the panel overlooked or misapprehended. No oral argument is allowed, and no response may be filed unless requested. In consolidated cases, one party’s petition does not count for another.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 31(d)(1)–(4), (6) (Prom. 2024-0001)"},{"t":"En banc: disfavored, with a strict opening statement","d":"En banc review is not favored. It is ordered only to keep decisions uniform or for a question of exceptional importance. The petition must open by stating either the conflict with controlling authority (with citations) or each exceptional question.\nA petition for initial hearing en banc is due by the date the appellee’s brief is due. A petition for rehearing en banc follows the Rule 31 time. Combined panel and en banc petitions count as one document for the 3,900-word limit.\nA vote is taken only if a justice calls for one. An en banc decision needs at least three justices. En banc is suspended when fewer than three active or senior justices are available.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 40(a)–(c), (f), (h), (i); 31(d)(7)"},{"t":"Panel decisions bind everyone","d":"A panel decision is binding precedent for all lower courts and for later panels. Only the en banc Court can overrule or modify a prior decision.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 31(c)"}]],["Mandate, costs, and further review",[{"t":"Mandate in 21 days","d":"The mandate issues 21 days after entry of judgment. A timely rehearing petition stays it. If the petition is denied, the mandate issues 10 days after the denial. Seeking further review does not stay the mandate, so move for a stay, which requires a substantial question and good cause and may require a bond.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 32(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Appellate costs and fees: 14 days","d":"File an itemized, verified bill of costs, including any attorney’s fees, within 14 days after entry of judgment. Untimely bills are denied unless a motion shows extraordinary circumstances. Objections are due 14 days after service, and an answer 14 days after that. The amount of attorney’s fees is fixed by the Superior Court on remand. Frivolous appeals can draw damages and single or double costs.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 30(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Review beyond the Supreme Court of the Virgin Islands","d":"Rule 32 still refers to certiorari to the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit as well as the U.S. Supreme Court, and says such review follows those courts’ rules. Federal legislation in 2012 (Pub. L. 112-226, amending 48 U.S.C. § 1613) ended Third Circuit certiorari review and sends review to the U.S. Supreme Court. The federal statute was not among the collected texts, so confirm the route and the 90-day certiorari deadline in the U.S. Supreme Court’s rules.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 32(b)–(c); 48 U.S.C. § 1613 (not collected)"}]],["E-filing in the Supreme Court",[{"t":"VIJEFS for everything; paper copies of briefs","d":"Notices of appeal, writ petitions, and other initiating documents are filed through VIJEFS. Motions and petitions need only the electronic original unless the Clerk asks for more.\nBriefs are e-filed, and seven paper copies following the cover-color rules must be submitted within seven days or as directed. Four paper copies of the appendix go with the briefs. A filing is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. Atlantic Standard Time. Each single file may be up to 25 MB.","c":"V.I. E-Filing R. 5(c), 9, 13(a)(1), (3)–(5)"},{"t":"Older Rule 40 (VISCEFS) is superseded","d":"The 2017 appellate compilation still contains a Rule 40 on the former VISCEFS system. It required paper copies within three days and allowed one business day to cure a rejected notice of appeal. The 2020 Interim E-Filing Rules state that they prevail over conflicting procedural rules, and Prom. 2024-0001 reassigned Rule 40 to en banc procedure. Follow the E-Filing Rules, and check with the Clerk on any cure period after a rejection.","c":"V.I. E-Filing R. 1; Prom. 2024-0001"},{"t":"Technical failure","d":"If a filing is late because of a failure of the court’s system, the court shall grant appropriate relief on satisfactory proof. For other technical problems, relief is discretionary. The rules do not extend any deadline.","c":"V.I. E-Filing R. 14(a)–(b)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Civil notice of appeal to the Supreme Court","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment or order on the Superior Court docket.\nFiled with the Supreme Court Clerk. A missing separate document does not toll.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(1), (9)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order on the Superior Court docket)","note":"Filed with the Supreme Court Clerk. A missing separate document does not toll."}},{"t":"Civil notice of appeal when the V.I. or U.S. government is a party","d":"60 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nAvailable to any party when the Government of the Virgin Islands, the United States, or an officer or agency of either is a party.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(1)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Available to any party when the Government of the Virgin Islands, the United States, or an officer or agency of either is a party."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal or other party’s notice","d":"14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular deadline, if later).","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(3)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after a tolling motion is decided","d":"30 days from entry of the order disposing of the last Rule 50/52/59 motion (or Rule 60 motion filed within 28 days).\nA motion not decided within 120 days of filing is deemed denied. Fee motions do not toll.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order disposing of the last Rule 50/52/59 motion)","note":"A motion not decided within 120 days of filing is deemed denied. Fee motions do not toll."}},{"t":"Post-judgment motions in Superior Court (JMOL, amend findings, new trial, alter or amend)","d":"28 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)). An opposition to new-trial affidavits is due 20 days after service (Rule 59(c)).","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)). An opposition to new-trial affidavits is due 20 days after service (Rule 59(c))."}},{"t":"Motion to extend appeal time (excusable neglect or good cause)","d":"30 days from expiration of the original appeal deadline.\nThe extension may run up to 30 days past the deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(8)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal deadline)","note":"The extension may run up to 30 days past the deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Motion to reopen appeal time (no notice of entry)","d":"90 days from entry of judgment (or 14 days after receiving notice, if earlier).\nIf granted, the time is reopened for 14 days.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(10)","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"If granted, the time is reopened for 14 days."}},{"t":"Petition for permission to appeal a certified interlocutory order","d":"10 days from entry of the order in Superior Court (14 days after an amended order adding the certification).\n2,600 words. The answer is due in 10 days.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 6(a)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order in Superior Court)","note":"2,600 words. The answer is due in 10 days."}},{"t":"Motion for expedited appeal (required in all interlocutory appeals)","d":"14 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nOpposition due within 10 days.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 5(e), 6(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Opposition due within 10 days."}},{"t":"Docket fee if not paid at filing","d":"14 days from docketing of the appeal.\nFee is $105 per the Clerk’s schedule. The Clerk may dismiss without notice.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 3(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (docketing of the appeal)","note":"Fee is $105 per the Clerk’s schedule. The Clerk may dismiss without notice."}},{"t":"Order transcripts (TPO) and file a statement of issues","d":"14 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or order resolving a tolling motion).\nThe appellee may designate more within 14 days after service of the TPO.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 10(b)(1)–(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"The appellee may designate more within 14 days after service of the TPO."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief and appendix","d":"40 days from completion of the record or issuance of the briefing schedule, whichever is earlier.\n7,800 words.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 25(a), 24(a)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (completion of the record or issuance of the briefing schedule, whichever is earlier)","note":"7,800 words."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\n7,800 words.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 25(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":"7,800 words."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\n3,900 words.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 25(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellee’s brief)","note":"3,900 words."}},{"t":"Response to a motion in the Supreme Court","d":"14 days from service of the motion.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 21(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion to extend a Supreme Court deadline","d":"5 days from working days before the deadline to be extended (file no later than this).\nServe at least 3 working days before. Opposition at least 1 working day before.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 17","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (working days before the deadline to be extended)","note":"Serve at least 3 working days before. Opposition at least 1 working day before."}},{"t":"Petition for panel rehearing or rehearing en banc","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\n3,900 words. No response unless requested.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 31(d)(1); 40(c)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"3,900 words. No response unless requested."}},{"t":"Bill of costs in the Supreme Court","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nObjections due 14 days after service.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 30(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Objections due 14 days after service."}},{"t":"Mandate issues","d":"21 days from entry of judgment (10 days after denial of a rehearing petition).\nSeeking further review does not stay the mandate.","c":"V.I. R. App. P. 32(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Seeking further review does not stay the mandate."}},{"t":"Appeal from Magistrate Division to a Superior Court judge","d":"14 days from entry of the magistrate judge’s judgment or order.\n780-word position statement. Fee or IFP motion due within 14 days.","c":"Super. Ct. R. 322(b)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the magistrate judge’s judgment or order)","note":"780-word position statement. Fee or IFP motion due within 14 days."}},{"t":"Petition for writ of review of an agency decision","d":"30 days from date of the decision (unless a statute sets another time).\nFiled in the Superior Court’s Appellate Division.","c":"V.I. R. Civ. P. 91(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date of the decision)","note":"Filed in the Superior Court’s Appellate Division."}}]]],"dl":[["Civil notice of appeal to the Supreme Court",30,"Entry of the judgment or order on the Superior Court docket","V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(1), (9)","Filed with the Supreme Court Clerk. A missing separate document does not toll."],["Civil notice of appeal when the V.I. or U.S. government is a party",60,"Entry of the judgment or order","V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(1)","Available to any party when the Government of the Virgin Islands, the United States, or an officer or agency of either is a party."],["Cross-appeal or other party’s notice",14,"Filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular deadline, if later)","V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(3)",""],["Notice of appeal after a tolling motion is decided",30,"Entry of the order disposing of the last Rule 50/52/59 motion (or Rule 60 motion filed within 28 days)","V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4)","A motion not decided within 120 days of filing is deemed denied. Fee motions do not toll."],["Post-judgment motions in Superior Court (JMOL, amend findings, new trial, alter or amend)",28,"Entry of judgment","V.I. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","Cannot be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)). An opposition to new-trial affidavits is due 20 days after service (Rule 59(c))."],["Motion to extend appeal time (excusable neglect or good cause)",30,"Expiration of the original appeal deadline","V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(8)","The extension may run up to 30 days past the deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later."],["Motion to reopen appeal time (no notice of entry)",90,"Entry of judgment (or 14 days after receiving notice, if earlier)","V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(10)","If granted, the time is reopened for 14 days."],["Petition for permission to appeal a certified interlocutory order",10,"Entry of the order in Superior Court (14 days after an amended order adding the certification)","V.I. R. App. P. 6(a)","2,600 words. The answer is due in 10 days."],["Motion for expedited appeal (required in all interlocutory appeals)",14,"Filing of the notice of appeal","V.I. R. App. P. 5(e), 6(b)","Opposition due within 10 days."],["Docket fee if not paid at filing",14,"Docketing of the appeal","V.I. R. App. P. 3(a)","Fee is $105 per the Clerk’s schedule. The Clerk may dismiss without notice."],["Order transcripts (TPO) and file a statement of issues",14,"Filing of the notice of appeal (or order resolving a tolling motion)","V.I. R. App. P. 10(b)(1)–(2)","The appellee may designate more within 14 days after service of the TPO."],["Appellant’s brief and appendix",40,"Completion of the record or issuance of the briefing schedule, whichever is earlier","V.I. R. App. P. 25(a), 24(a)","7,800 words."],["Appellee’s brief",30,"Service of the appellant’s brief","V.I. R. App. P. 25(a)","7,800 words."],["Reply brief",14,"Service of the appellee’s brief","V.I. R. App. P. 25(a)","3,900 words."],["Response to a motion in the Supreme Court",14,"Service of the motion","V.I. R. App. P. 21(a)",""],["Motion to extend a Supreme Court deadline",5,"Working days before the deadline to be extended (file no later than this)","V.I. R. App. P. 17","Serve at least 3 working days before. Opposition at least 1 working day before."],["Petition for panel rehearing or rehearing en banc",14,"Entry of judgment","V.I. R. App. P. 31(d)(1); 40(c)","3,900 words. No response unless requested."],["Bill of costs in the Supreme Court",14,"Entry of judgment","V.I. R. App. P. 30(b)","Objections due 14 days after service."],["Mandate issues",21,"Entry of judgment (10 days after denial of a rehearing petition)","V.I. R. App. P. 32(a)","Seeking further review does not stay the mandate."],["Appeal from Magistrate Division to a Superior Court judge",14,"Entry of the magistrate judge’s judgment or order","Super. Ct. R. 322(b)(1)","780-word position statement. Fee or IFP motion due within 14 days."],["Petition for writ of review of an agency decision",30,"Date of the decision (unless a statute sets another time)","V.I. R. Civ. P. 91(a)","Filed in the Superior Court’s Appellate Division."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.vicourts.org/"]]},"rulesName":"Virgin Islands Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope and purpose","s":"The rules govern all civil actions in the Superior Court, except where other Supreme Court rules or the law provide otherwise. They are to be construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. Specialized rule sets (small claims, family, probate, habeas) govern their own case types.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"1-1","t":"Title, citation and application","s":"Cite the rules as “V.I. R. Civ. P.” They apply to actions filed after their effective date and to pending actions, unless the Supreme Court orders otherwise or the Superior Court expressly finds that applying them would be infeasible or unjust.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"1-2","t":"Definitions; magistrate judges","s":"Defines “judge” (appointed under 4 V.I.C. § 72(a) or designated by statute) and “magistrate judge” (appointed under 4 V.I.C. § 122(a)). Wherever the rules say “judge,” a magistrate judge may exercise that authority unless it conflicts with the rules, precedent, or a statute.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"1-3","t":"Procedure where no rule or Virgin Islands law controls","s":"Where no rule, Supreme Court precedent, or Code provision prescribes a procedure, the judge may regulate practice consistent with Virgin Islands law. No sanction may be imposed for breaking an unwritten requirement unless the court first gave the parties actual notice of it by order.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action: the civil action.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencing an action","s":"A civil action begins by filing a complaint with the clerk. Under the Reporter’s Note, filing fees must be paid in advance under 4 V.I.C. §§ 511–512, and in forma pauperis status is governed by 4 V.I.C. § 513, not by the rules.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"3-1","t":"Contact information; case information form","s":"Every attorney and self-represented party must give a phone number, mailing address, and e-mail under the signature on every filing, and must immediately file and serve a notice of any change. In e-filed cases, VIJEFS registration and updates satisfy this (amended eff. Jan. 1, 2021). The plaintiff files a Case Information and Litigant Data Form with the complaint, and each other party files one with its first appearance.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons and service of process","s":"Any non-party adult (18+) or the V.I. Marshal may serve. Service methods:\n• Individuals in the territory: personal delivery, abode service, an agent, or a court-ordered alternative after diligent attempts.\n• Individuals outside the territory: 5 V.I.C. ch. 503 and § 4911.\n• LLCs: may be served through a member.\n• Government of the Virgin Islands: special rules, including personal service on the Executive Director of the Legislature or the Administrator of Courts in suits over Legislative or Judicial Branch acts.\n• United States: Rule 4(j), added 2019.\nDifferences from the federal rule: Rule 4(d) is reserved, so there is no waiver-of-service procedure. The time to serve under 4(n) is 120 days, not 90. Rule 4(o) treats process actually received in time as sufficient even if not properly served (not for divorce or annulment), with the plaintiff bearing the burden to prove timely receipt. Proof of service other than by the Marshal is by affidavit under 5 V.I.C. § 114. Rule 4(m)(2) was revised in 2023 so out-of-territory service is proved under 5 V.I.C. § 4911.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"4-1","t":"Service by publication","s":"Publication is available only when Rule 4 service cannot be made and the affidavit requirements of 5 V.I.C. § 112(a) are met. The published summons must contain what § 112(b) requires, and the court’s order controls which newspapers are used under § 112(c).","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Serving other process","s":"Process other than a summons, such as contempt-type and some in rem process, is served by the Marshal or a person specially appointed. Ordinary civil service follows Rule 4, and later papers follow Rule 5.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"5","t":"Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","s":"Every paper after the complaint must be served on every party. In cases governed by the Electronic Filing Rules, filing through VIJEFS and the electronic notice accomplish service, and proof of service follows E-Filing Rule 7(c) (amended eff. Jan. 1, 2021).\nOtherwise, permitted methods are hand delivery, leaving at the office or abode, mail (complete on mailing), leaving with the V.I. Marshal if the person has no known address, or electronic means with written consent. Discovery and Rule 26(a)(1)–(2) disclosures are not filed until used.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Constitutional challenge to a statute","s":"A party challenging a Virgin Islands statute must file a notice and serve the Attorney General, and the court must certify the challenge. The Government may intervene within 60 days after the notice or certification, whichever is earlier. A failure to give notice does not forfeit the claim.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"5.2","t":"Privacy protection for filings","s":"Filers must redact Social Security and taxpayer ID numbers and financial-account numbers (last four digits only), birth dates (year only), and minors’ names (initials only), unless the court orders otherwise. The exemptions and the sealed or reference-list options track the federal rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"6","t":"Computing and extending time","s":"Differences from the federal rule:\n• For periods of 14 days or less, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays are excluded. Periods of 15 days or more count every day.\n• Legal holidays are the Virgin Islands list (including Three Kings Day, Transfer Day, Holy Thursday, Easter Monday, Juneteenth (added 2023), Emancipation Day, D. Hamilton Jackson Day, Election Day, and Christmas Second Day).\n• 3 days are added after service by mail, by leaving with the Marshal, or by other consented means.\nExtensions require good cause or excusable neglect, judged on listed factors. The deadlines in Rules 50(b) and (d), 52(b), 59(b), (d), and (e), and 60(b) cannot be extended.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"6-1","t":"Motion requirements, length, and timing","s":"Motions must be in writing, state their grounds with authority, and attach a proposed order if they seek affirmative relief. Only a motion, response, and reply are allowed without leave. Supplemental affidavits are due at least 10 days before the hearing, and responsive ones at least 5 days before.\nUse at least 12-point font. Each paper is limited to the greater of 20 pages or 6,000 words, and the certificate of service must state compliance. Responses are due in 14 days (20 days for Rule 12 motions; Rule 56 has its own schedule), and replies within 14 days after the response.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"6-2","t":"Motions for extension of time","s":"An extension motion must state that the movant conferred with opposing parties, and their position, or that their position could not be learned despite diligence. It must also report how many extensions the movant has already sought for the same deadline.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"6-3","t":"Continuances","s":"Continuances of trials, conferences, and hearings require good cause, by written motion or stipulation filed at the earliest practical date and not less than 7 days before the event, except in exceptional circumstances. Requests based on a missing witness or a counsel conflict must make specific showings, including alternative dates. Supreme Court Rule 111 (eff. July 1, 2026) adds that the request must be signed by both attorney and party, and it lists what is and is not good cause.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"6-4","t":"Motions for reconsideration","s":"A motion to reconsider a non-final order is due within 14 days after entry, extendable only for good cause. Final judgments are governed by Rules 59 and 60. Grounds are limited to an intervening change in law, new evidence, clear error of law, or the court’s failure to address an issue actually raised, and in that last case the movant must cite where it was raised.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"6-5","t":"Withdrawal of counsel","s":"Counsel may withdraw only with leave of court after notice to the client. The motion must include a verified statement about contacting the client, proof or attempts of service on the client, and updated litigant data forms with the client’s last known address and phone number.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"6-6","t":"Stay for National Guard members","s":"Added effective Jan. 1, 2021, under 15 V.I.C. § 1519(c). A Guard member called up by the Governor may move to stay any civil case, with copies of the orders and an Adjutant General statement. The court must stay the case for 30 consecutive days unless the member’s ability to proceed is not materially affected, and may require recertification every 30 days.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; motions","s":"Lists the permitted pleadings, with a reply to an answer only if ordered. Requests for orders are made by motion under Rules 6-1 to 6-5.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"7.1","t":"Disclosure statement","s":"A nongovernmental corporate party, or a corporation seeking to intervene, must file two copies of a statement identifying any parent and any public company owning 10% or more of its stock, or stating there is none. It must be filed with the first appearance or filing and supplemented promptly (as amended eff. Sept. 1, 2023).","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Rule 8(a)(2) says expressly that the Virgin Islands is a notice-pleading jurisdiction. The Reporter’s Note says this departs from the federal plausibility standard. Claims go in numbered paragraphs with separate counts. Under 5 V.I.C. § 5, the prayer must say damages are within the court’s jurisdictional limit and may not state a dollar amount. Rules for denials and affirmative defenses track the federal rule, and Rule 8(b)(3) (general denials) is reserved.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"8-1","t":"Fictitious-name (‘John Doe’) defendants","s":"Process may issue against a defendant described by a fictitious name when the true name is unknown. Before judgment, the pleadings must be amended on motion and notice, with an affidavit showing how the true name was learned. No affidavit is needed if the defendant acknowledged the name.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Tracks the federal rule. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity. Capacity is raised by specific denial. Conditions precedent, official documents, judgments, time and place, and special damages are handled as in federal practice.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Requires a caption, numbered paragraphs, and incorporation by reference, with exhibits forming part of the pleading. A jury demand endorsed on a pleading goes on the front page under the title (“Demand For Jury Trial”), but failing to use that form is not a waiver. Rule 10(e), added 2018, lets any party designate or counter-designate a case as “Complex” in the caption of its initial pleading.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing papers; representations; sanctions","s":"Tracks federal Rule 11, including the 21-day safe harbor for sanctions motions. Virgin Islands addition, Rule 11(b)(5): signing also certifies that the applicable Virgin Islands law has been cited, including authority for and against the party’s position.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections","s":"Answer deadlines:\n• 21 days if served in the territory.\n• 30 days if served outside it under 5 V.I.C. § 4911.\n• 30 days after publication ends, or after mail receipt, for service under 5 V.I.C. § 112(c).\n• 30 days for the Government of the Virgin Islands and its agencies and official-capacity officers, after service on the Governor and Attorney General.\n• 45 days for government employees sued individually.\n• 21 days for counterclaims and crossclaims.\nAfter a denied motion, or after a more definite statement is served, the answer is due in 14 days. A motion to strike is due within 21 days, and a more definite statement must be given within 14 days of the order. Waiver and consolidation of Rule 12(b) defenses follow the federal rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and crossclaim","s":"Tracks the federal rule. Counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory, others are permissive, and crossclaims must relate to the original transaction.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may implead a non-party who may be liable to it without leave if it files within 14 days after serving its original answer. After that, leave is required. In complex cases, fourth- and further-party complaints need an express finding (Rule 94).","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as of course no later than 21 days after serving the pleading, or 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or Rule 12(b), (e), or (f) motion, whichever is earlier (“no later than” added 2023). Otherwise, amendment needs consent or leave, freely given. A response to an amended pleading is due within the time left to answer the original or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Relation back follows the federal rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"15-1","t":"Form, filing and effect of amendments","s":"A motion to amend must attach a complete, signed copy of the proposed pleading. Every amendment must restate the whole pleading, show the changes, and be labeled (for example, FIRST AMENDED COMPLAINT), without incorporating earlier pleadings by reference. A pleading filed with leave is deemed filed on the date the motion was filed.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"15-2","t":"Amendment by the court","s":"The court may amend any process or pleading for an omission, defect, or variance from the evidence. If a party is surprised, the court may adjourn the hearing for an appropriate period.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"In Track 1–3 cases under the Superior Court’s differentiated case-management program, the judge must issue a scheduling order after the Rule 26(f) report or a conference. Absent good cause, the order is due within the earlier of 90 days after any defendant is served or 60 days after any defendant appears. Final pretrial conference and sanctions provisions track the federal rule. Supreme Court Rule 110 (eff. July 1, 2026) adds disposition time guidelines, such as 540 days for most civil cases and 1,460 days for Complex Litigation Division cases.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"16-1","t":"Joint final pretrial order","s":"The parties prepare a single joint final pretrial order. The plaintiff convenes a conference to reconcile disagreements and files the signed order. Sanctions may enforce it. Sample Form 16-A (joint discovery and scheduling plan) uses defaults of 25 interrogatories, 25 document requests, 25 requests for admission, 10 depositions, and 7-hour single-day depositions, and calls for Rule 90 mediation.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"17","t":"Real party in interest; capacity; public officers","s":"Tracks the federal rule. Capacity is determined by Virgin Islands law. A minor or incompetent person without a representative may sue by next friend without a court appointment.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims","s":"A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, including alternative and contingent claims.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"19","t":"Required joinder of parties","s":"Tracks federal Rule 19. A person needed for complete relief or to protect an interest must be joined if feasible. If not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if the claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence and share a common question. The court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice. The word “admiralty” was struck from 20(a)(2) in 2018.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal. The court may add or drop parties, or sever claims, on just terms.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A plaintiff or defendant exposed to double or multiple liability may require claimants to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the stakeholder denies liability.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Tracks federal Rule 23 for prerequisites, types, notice, and class counsel. Settlement approval in 23(e), as amended eff. Jan. 1, 2021, requires reasonable notice, a hearing, a finding that the deal is fair, reasonable, and adequate, and disclosure of side agreements. It also allows a second opt-out. Rule 23(f) (interlocutory appeal of certification) is reserved, and every putative class action is automatically designated complex under 23(d)(3) (added 2018).","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions","s":"A shareholder or member may sue on the entity’s behalf. The complaint must be verified, allege ownership at the time of the transaction, and state with particularity the demand made or why none was made. Settlement or dismissal requires court approval and notice.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"Members may sue or be sued as representatives of an unincorporated association only if they will fairly and adequately protect its interests. Rule 23(d) and (e) procedures apply.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right and permissive intervention track the federal rule, with express provision for the Government of the Virgin Islands and its agencies, boards, and officers. The motion must state its grounds and attach a pleading.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"Difference from the federal rule: after a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute may be granted any time within 2 years after the death, not 90 days after a statement noting the death. Substitution for incompetency, transfer of interest, and successors in public office otherwise track the federal rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"26","t":"Duty to disclose; general discovery provisions","s":"Disclosure deadlines:\n• Initial disclosures are due within 14 days after the 26(f) conference, or 30 days after joinder for later parties.\n• Expert disclosures with written reports (publications from the past 10 years, testimony from the past 4) are due at least 90 days before trial. Rebuttal experts are due within 30 days after the other side’s disclosure.\n• Pretrial disclosures are due 30 days before trial, with objections 14 days later.\nThe parties confer at least 21 days before the scheduling conference or order. Early Rule 34 requests may be delivered more than 21 days after service.\nDifferences from the federal rule: the scope of discovery is any nonprivileged matter relevant to a claim or defense, with no proportionality language in 26(b)(1). Initial disclosures require producing copies of documents, not just describing them, and include insurance agreements. Depositions of identified experts are a matter of right.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"26-1","t":"Expert witness fees and testimony","s":"Expert deposition fees are paid under Rule 26(b)(4)(E) unless agreed otherwise. A proposed bill must be given a reasonable time in advance, and disputes go to the court before the deposition. The deposing party must advance any fee demanded in advance. An expert’s trial testimony is limited to opinions in the report or supplement and those elicited at deposition.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions to perpetuate testimony","s":"A verified petition may be filed before suit, or a motion made pending appeal, to preserve testimony. The petition and notice of hearing must be served at least 21 days before the hearing.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Within the territory, a deposition is taken before an officer authorized to give oaths by Virgin Islands law or the law of the place. Outside the territory, depositions must comply with 5 V.I.C. § 4921. Related or interested persons are disqualified.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations about discovery procedure","s":"Parties may stipulate to deposition and discovery procedures. Court approval is needed only if a stipulation would interfere with court-ordered deadlines.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"29-1","t":"Stipulations expanding disclosures","s":"The parties may stipulate to expand the scope of Rule 26(a) disclosures.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"Leave is needed for more than 10 depositions per side or a repeat deposition. A deposition is limited to 1 day of 7 hours unless extended. Objections must be concise and non-suggestive, and only one attorney per party may question unless stipulated or ordered. The deponent has 30 days to review. The officer seals the transcript and sends it to the attorney who arranged it.\nThe organizational-deposition rule (30(b)(6), amended 2023) requires the parties to confer about the topics, as the federal rule does. Rule 30(i) points to letters rogatory (5 V.I.C. § 4921) and the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act (5 V.I.C. § 4922 et seq.).","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"Cross-questions are due within 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions, redirect within 7 days, and recross within 7 days. Leave is needed beyond 10 depositions or for a repeat deposition.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"32","t":"Using depositions in court","s":"Tracks the federal rule. A witness is unavailable if, among other grounds, more than 100 miles away. A deposition may not be used against a party who got less than 14 days’ notice and promptly sought a protective order that was still pending. Objections to the form of written questions must be served within the time for responsive questions, or 7 days for recross.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories","s":"A party may serve no more than 25 interrogatories, including discrete subparts, absent stipulation or leave. Answers are due in 30 days, under oath. The business-records option is available only if the party represents in good faith that it cannot otherwise answer with reasonable effort, and it must produce copies of the records with the answer unless duplication is unduly burdensome.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"34","t":"Producing documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","s":"Tracks the federal rule. Responses are due in 30 days, or 30 days after the first 26(f) conference for early requests. Objections must state whether anything is being withheld, and ESI form objections follow the federal rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"An examination requires a court order on good cause, or the parties’ agreement. The order sets time, place, manner, scope, examiner, and who may attend or record. Examinations expressly include DNA testing where it is in controversy. Report exchange tracks the federal rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days. Denials must fairly meet the substance, and lack of knowledge requires a reasonable inquiry. Admissions may be withdrawn or amended on motion.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to disclose or cooperate; sanctions","s":"Tracks federal Rule 37. Motions to compel need a good-faith conferral certification, and evasive answers count as failures. Rule 37(e) on lost ESI allows an adverse inference or dispositive sanctions only on a finding of intent to deprive.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"37-1","t":"Pre-motion discovery conference","s":"Before any discovery motion other than one about depositions, the movant must serve a letter listing each disputed request, its position, and the order sought. Counsel must then confer in person if practicable, otherwise by phone or video (mail or e-mail is not enough), within 15 days after the letter.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"38","t":"Right to jury trial; demand","s":"The jury right is preserved under the Seventh Amendment and the Revised Organic Act. A written demand is due no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue, and other parties may demand on other issues within 14 days after service. A party that does not properly serve and file a demand waives the right.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or there is no jury right. Without a demand, the court may still order a jury on motion. Advisory juries are allowed.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"40","t":"[Reserved]","s":"No rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"Voluntary dismissal without order is allowed before an answer or summary judgment motion, or by stipulation. A second dismissal of the same claim, after an earlier federal or Virgin Islands dismissal, operates as an adjudication on the merits. Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or comply operates on the merits unless the order says otherwise. The court may award the costs of a previously dismissed action.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"The court may consolidate actions sharing a common question, or order separate trials of issues or claims, preserving the jury right.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking testimony","s":"Testimony is taken orally in open court unless a statute or rule provides otherwise. Motions may be heard on affidavits. Parties needing an interpreter must submit qualified interpreters’ names for approval at least 6 days before the services are needed. Interpreter pay is subject to 4 V.I.C. § 323.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"44","t":"Proving an official record","s":"Proof of official records follows 5 V.I.C. ch. 509 and Articles VIII–IX of the Virgin Islands Rules of Evidence.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determining foreign law","s":"Proof of other jurisdictions’ law follows 5 V.I.C. ch. 507.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Tracks the federal rule, with these differences:\n• Notice and a copy of a pretrial document subpoena must be served on each party at least 5 days before the subpoena is served.\n• Any non-party adult may serve.\n• Attendance is limited to within 100 miles.\n• Objections are due before the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days after service.\nOut-of-territory subpoenas use 5 V.I.C. § 4922 et seq. (amended 2018) and letters rogatory under § 4921.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"46","t":"Objecting to a ruling","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary. It is enough to state the action wanted or the objection and its grounds.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"47","t":"Selecting jurors","s":"The court or the attorneys may examine prospective jurors, and if the court examines, it must allow further inquiry. Peremptory challenges are governed by 5 V.I.C. § 323. Jurors may be excused for good cause.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"47-1","t":"Juror contact","s":"No attorney, party, or witness may communicate, directly or indirectly, with prospective or sitting jurors before or during trial. After trial, no contact is allowed until the jury is discharged without the court’s permission (as amended 2023).","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"48","t":"Number of jurors; verdict; polling","s":"The number of jurors, verdicts, and polling are governed by 4 V.I.C. § 80 and 5 V.I.C. chapters 31 and 33, not set in the rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts; general verdict with questions","s":"Tracks the federal rule, including how inconsistencies between answers and a general verdict are handled. Issues not submitted are deemed waived or found by the court.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law; new trial","s":"JMOL may be made after a party is fully heard. A renewed motion is due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, or after the jury is discharged if an issue was not decided by verdict, and may be joined with a Rule 59 request. A new-trial motion by a party against whom JMOL is entered is due within 28 days.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"51","t":"Jury instructions; objections","s":"Requests are filed at the close of evidence or earlier as ordered. They must be marked with the case name and number, cite authority, and be numbered in sequence. The court must disclose proposed instructions before argument, and objections must be made on the record to preserve error.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings and conclusions by the court","s":"In a non-jury case, the court finds facts specially. The parties must file proposed findings and conclusions within 21 days after trial ends. A motion to amend findings is due no later than 28 days after judgment. Judgment on partial findings is available.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Masters may be appointed by consent or for exceptional needs, after notice. They must be free of 4 V.I.C. § 284 disqualifying ties unless the parties consent, and may impose Rule 37 and 45 sanctions. Objections or motions to adopt or modify a master’s order or report are due no later than 21 days after service. The Complex Litigation Division may appoint masters under Rule 95.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs; attorney’s fees","s":"Rule 54(b) partial final judgment requires an express no-just-reason-for-delay finding.\nThe Virgin Islands differences are in costs and fees:\n• The prevailing party must serve and file a bill of costs within 30 days after judgment. It must be itemized, verified by affidavit under 5 V.I.C. § 543(b), and supported by invoices. Noncompliance may waive costs.\n• Under 5 V.I.C. § 541, the court may award the prevailing party attorney’s fees as indemnity. Fees are barred in personal-injury cases unless the claim or defense was frivolous.\n• Fee issues may be referred to a magistrate judge or master.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"55","t":"Default; default judgment","s":"The clerk or court enters default on a showing of failure to plead or defend. Judgment for a sum certain may be entered on affidavit. Otherwise, apply to the court, with 7 days’ written notice of the hearing to a party that has appeared. Defaults are set aside for good cause or under Rule 60(b). A default judgment against the Government of the Virgin Islands, its officers, or agencies requires the claimant to establish the claim by evidence that satisfies the court.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"55-1","t":"Default judgment applications","s":"The court may require notice to the defaulting party when justice requires. In deficiency suits or claims tied to a repossessed chattel, the plaintiff must prove a description of the property, the sale amount or credit, and the cost of sale.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"Unless the court sets another time, a motion may be filed until 30 days after all discovery closes.\nThe motion must include a separately numbered statement of undisputed facts with record citations, which does not count toward the 20-page or 6,000-word limit. The opposition is due within 30 days of filing. It must respond to each numbered fact and may add numbered disputed facts. The reply is due within 14 days and must answer the added facts (adopted 2019).","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgment","s":"Procedure for declaratory judgments under 5 V.I.C. ch. 89. A jury may be demanded, other adequate remedies do not bar relief, and a speedy hearing may be ordered.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"58","t":"Entering judgment","s":"Every judgment needs a separate document, except orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 54 fees, 59, and 60 motions. The judge signs promptly and the clerk enters it, and the judgment takes effect for appeal on entry. Under V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(9), appeal time runs from docket entry even without a separate document.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"59","t":"New trial; altering or amending a judgment","s":"Motions for a new trial and to alter or amend are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, and opposing affidavits are due 20 days after service. The court may order a new trial on its own within 28 days. The Reporter’s Note says remittitur is not valid in the Virgin Islands.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time, but only with the Supreme Court’s leave while an appeal is docketed. Rule 60(b) grounds track the federal rule. A motion must be made within a reasonable time, and within one year for mistake, newly discovered evidence, and fraud. Under V.I. R. App. P. 5(a)(4), only a Rule 60 motion filed within 28 days tolls appeal time.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors that do not affect a party’s substantial rights are not grounds for a new trial or for disturbing a judgment.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"A judgment is automatically stayed for 30 days after entry, as amended in 2019 to match the federal rule. Injunctions and receiverships are not stayed. The court may stay pending post-trial motions on security, and a party may obtain a stay at any time by bond. The Government of the Virgin Islands is exempt from bonds to the extent of 5 V.I.C. § 1141. The Supreme Court’s powers under V.I. R. App. P. 8 are preserved.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"63","t":"Judge’s inability to proceed","s":"A successor judge may continue after certifying familiarity with the record. In non-jury matters, the judge must recall material disputed witnesses on request if they are available.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizing a person or property","s":"Every Virgin Islands provisional remedy for seizing a person or property to secure a potential judgment is available from the start of the action.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions and restraining orders","s":"A preliminary injunction requires notice, and the hearing may be consolidated with trial. A TRO without notice expires within the time set, not more than 14 days, and may be extended once for good cause. The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. The movant must give security in an amount the court considers proper, but the Government of the Virgin Islands, its officers, and agencies need not give security.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Proceedings against a security provider","s":"A security provider (surety) submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the clerk as agent, and liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action (terminology amended 2019).","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"The rules govern receivership actions. An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order (added 2018).","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit into court","s":"A party may deposit money or a thing on notice and with leave. Deposits and withdrawals follow 5 V.I.C. § 1391.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"Unlike the federal rule, either side may make the offer, at least 14 days before trial. Acceptance must come within 14 days. An unaccepted offer is withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs. After a liability finding, an offer may be made at least 14 days before the damages hearing. Cost-shifting applies only as allowed by 5 V.I.C. § 541 and other statutes.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution on judgments","s":"Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution under Virgin Islands law. Examination of the judgment debtor follows 5 V.I.C. §§ 501–502, and certified orders for asset discovery must be served personally at least 7 days before the appearance date.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"70","t":"Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","s":"If a party fails to perform a specific act, the court may appoint another person to do it at that party’s expense, enter a judgment vesting title to property located in the territory, issue a writ of execution or assistance on a judgment for possession, and hold the party in contempt.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"71","t":"Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","s":"An order for or against a nonparty is enforced by the same procedure as for a party.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"71.1","t":"Condemning real or personal property","s":"Eminent-domain procedure adapted from the federal rule. The notice tells defendants they may answer within 21 days after service, and objections or defenses must be raised by answer within 21 days. Issues are tried before commissioners.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"72–76","t":"[Reserved]","s":"No rules.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"77","t":"Conducting judicial business","s":"The court is always open for emergency filings. Regular sessions are held at Charlotte Amalie (St. Thomas/St. John) and Kingshill (St. Croix). The clerk serves notice of entry of orders and judgments, but lack of notice does not extend appeal time except as V.I. R. App. P. 5(a) allows.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"77-1","t":"Advancing a case on the calendar","s":"For good cause, the court may advance the trial of any contested case.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"77-2","t":"Cases not reached","s":"Contested matters not reached on the trial date carry over until a new date is set.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"78","t":"Hearing motions","s":"The court may set regular times and places for motion hearings.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"79","t":"Records kept by the clerk","s":"The clerk keeps calendars, the civil docket, and records of judgments and orders.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"79-1","t":"Sealing of records","s":"The court may seal the entire record of a case it deems sensitive. A case-number cross-reference stays public.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"79-2","t":"Exemplification of court records","s":"Parties may obtain attested copies with an exemplification (the clerk’s exemplification, the judge’s certificate, and the clerk’s certificate) for a fee the court sets. The fixed $50 fee was removed in 2018.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"79-3","t":"Exhibits","s":"The clerk keeps exhibits through any appeal, while proponents keep sensitive exhibits such as narcotics, weapons, and currency. Offering parties must claim exhibits within 30 days after the clerk’s written request. Exhibits unclaimed 120 days after trial and any appeal may be destroyed.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"80","t":"Record of proceedings","s":"The reporter-secretary prepares certified transcripts on request at court-set rates, and the clerk provides electronic transcripts of digital recordings on request.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"81","t":"[Reserved]","s":"No rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit the Superior Court’s jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"83","t":"[Reserved]","s":"No rule.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"84","t":"Unsworn declarations under penalty of perjury","s":"An unsworn declaration under penalty of perjury, in the prescribed form, may substitute for an affidavit or verification, except for depositions and oaths of office.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"85","t":"Court orders","s":"Orders and judgments must be written and signed by the judge, magistrate judge, or clerk. Oral orders are enforceable until reduced to writing.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"86–89","t":"[Reserved]","s":"No rules.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"90","t":"Civil mediation","s":"Any civil matter may be referred except criminal cases, agency appeals, forfeitures, and habeas and extraordinary writs.\n• Within 14 days after referral, the parties notify the court of the mediator and date, or the plaintiff reports there is no agreement and the court appoints one.\n• The first session is held within 60 days after referral.\n• Each party must tell the others at least 5 days before who will attend.\nParties must attend personally or by a representative with full settlement authority, including each insurer through a non-outside-counsel representative. Unexcused non-attendance or bad faith requires sanctions, including mediator fees and other parties’ attorney’s fees. Remote mediation by video is allowed. These provisions were amended eff. Sept. 1, 2023.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"91","t":"Writs of review","s":"Petitions to review decisions of statutory officers, boards, commissions, or tribunals are filed in the Appellate Division within the statutory time, or 30 days after the decision if none is set. Counsel must certify the decision is erroneous and the petition is not for delay, and a bond may be required.\nThe respondent returns the writ and certified record within 20 days. The petitioner’s brief is due 20 days later and the respondent’s brief 20 days after that, with a reply only if ordered. Unless a statute provides otherwise, facts are reviewed for clear error and legal rulings get plenary review.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"92","t":"Complex cases","s":"Adopted eff. Oct. 1, 2018; not in the 2019 compilation. Presumptively complex cases include multi-party environmental, mass, or toxic torts, construction or product defect claims, natural-disaster claims, securities claims, class actions, and related insurance-coverage claims. Cases designated in a pleading, or referred by the assigned judge, go to the Complex Litigation Division judge, who decides whether to keep them. Assignment by the Presiding Judge in a written order considers listed factors (amended 2019), and a substitute judge applies these rules if the division judge recuses.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"93","t":"Centralized management in the Complex Litigation Division","s":"The division judge may designate related cases, open master cases with their own dockets, and require filings in the master or individual case. Grouping does not consolidate cases without an express order. The first case-management conference is held within 60 days after the master case opens, and a joint pre-conference statement is due 21 days before it. A case-management order issues within 28 days after each conference. Objections are due within 14 days, or they are waived.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"94","t":"Crossclaims, counterclaims, third-party actions in complex cases","s":"Rules 7–15-2 apply, but fourth- and further-party complaints are not of right and need an express finding that they will not delay the case. They are filed in the individual case, and in the master case only if directed.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"95","t":"Masters in the Complex Litigation Division","s":"Because complex cases are inherently exceptional, the division judge may appoint masters for individual, related, or master cases, with powers set by the appointment order consistent with Rule 53.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"},{"n":"96","t":"Ending centralized management","s":"If centralized management is no longer warranted for a category of cases, the division judge must issue a written explanation and a report on transfers, followed by a notice-and-comment process. A master’s authority ends on that determination unless extended.","u":"https://www.vicourts.org/rules"}]});
